Read the graph: $f$ rises from large negative values, crosses the $x$-axis, reaches its maximum $n$, falls, crosses the $x$-axis again, reaches its minimum $m<0$ and then increases towards the $x$-axis without reaching it. Count the intersections of the horizontal line $y=a$ with the curve:

- $a>n$: no root; $a=n$: one root (the maximum);
- $0<a<n$: one root on the rising branch and one on the falling branch, none on the last branch (which stays below $0$): exactly two roots;
- $m<a<0$: one root on the first rising branch, one on the falling branch below the axis and one on the final rising branch: three roots;
- $a<m$: one root.

So $0<a<n$ gives exactly two roots. Option A includes $a\ge n$ (zero or one root), option B includes $a=n$ (only one root), and option D gives three roots. **Answer: C.**
